      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $4{z}^{2}+7z-1=3{z}^{2}+3z-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}+7z-1 & =3{z}^{2}+3z-1 \\\\ (4{z}^{2}+7z-1)-(3{z}^{2}+3z-1) & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+7z-1-3{z}^{2}-3z+1 & =0 \\\\ (4{z}^{2}-3{z}^{2})+(7z-3z)+(-1+1) & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}+4z & =0 \\\\ z(z+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\nz=0\\,\\text{or}\\,z+4=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nz=0\\,\\text{or}\\,z=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=0,-4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
